## Reminder — when to show it

Show a short warning **if both of these are true**:

1. This turn’s work is clearly for a sibling project (e.g. EP), **and**
2. This agent run’s cwd / `--workspace` is **not** that project root (e.g. still `/path/to/Cuttle`).

**Still warn even if** you manually read that project’s rules/skills this turn — loading them compensates for behavior, it does **not** fix cwd. The reminder is about the wrong workspace, not about whether you remembered to open the runbook.

**Do not warn** when:

- The reply banner already shows cwd under `Escape-Purgatory` (or the sibling root), **or**
- The user is only asking about Cuttle itself.

### Wording

If the chat project chip is already the sibling project but agent cwd is still Cuttle:

```markdown
> [!WARNING]
> **Agent cwd is still Cuttle** — the chat project chip may say Escape Purgatory, but this `/cursor` run used workspace `/path/to/Cuttle`, so EP rules/skills did not auto-load. Open `/path/to/Escape-Purgatory` in Cursor (or confirm the chat is sending that `project_path`).
```

Only if the chip/cwd situation is unknown / clearly Cuttle-only:

```markdown
> [!WARNING]
> **Cursor workspace is Cuttle** — Escape Purgatory rules/skills did not auto-load for this run. Open `/path/to/Escape-Purgatory` for EP sessions when you want them by default.
```

Keep it to 1–2 short lines. Aliases: `[!WORKSPACE]` / `[!CAUTION]` (same amber style).

## Don'ts

- Don't assume missing skills mean the feature doesn't exist — check sibling project roots.
- Don't skip Discord/format runbooks for EP just because Cuttle is open.
- Don't leave the user thinking Cuttle rules alone govern EP (or other) work.
- Don't tell the user their **Cuttle chat project chip** is “wrong” when it already says Escape Purgatory.
- Don't paste the warning on every EP reply out of habit — only when agent cwd is not the sibling project root.
- Don't skip the warning just because you manually opened EP rules this turn — still warn when cwd is Cuttle.
- Don't use a plain gray `>` quote for this reminder — use `[!WARNING]` / `[!WORKSPACE]`.
